WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.588
Go applications often run as services,
command-line tools, workers,

2
00:00:03.668 --> 00:00:04.767
or internal automation.

3
00:00:04.847 --> 00:00:07.673
Each form needs careful handling of
secrets and sensitive data.

4
00:00:07.753 --> 00:00:10.945
API keys, tokens, database passwords,
private keys, signing material,

5
00:00:11.025 --> 00:00:14.689
connection strings, and privileged service
credentials should not live in source

6
00:00:14.769 --> 00:00:18.432
code, sample files, container images,
crash output, or client-facing responses.

7
00:00:18.512 --> 00:00:22.123
Configuration should come from controlled
sources such as deployment settings,

8
00:00:22.203 --> 00:00:24.611
secret managers, or approved runtime
configuration.

9
00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:28.406
The application should validate required
settings during startup and fail clearly

10
00:00:28.486 --> 00:00:30.946
when security-relevant values are missing
or invalid.

11
00:00:31.026 --> 00:00:33.590
A missing secret, empty allowlist,
disabled TLS setting,

12
00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:37.282
or unexpected debug mode should not
silently become normal production behavior.

13
00:00:37.362 --> 00:00:39.350
Logs and errors need intentional
redaction.

14
00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:42.623
Operators need enough information to
understand failures, but tokens,

15
00:00:42.703 --> 00:00:45.215
keys, passwords, private user data, raw
request bodies,

16
00:00:45.295 --> 00:00:48.487
and sensitive records should not be copied
into logs, metrics, tickets,

17
00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:49.404
or alert payloads.

18
00:00:49.484 --> 00:00:53.148
Structured logging can help by separating
event fields and making redaction rules

19
00:00:53.228 --> 00:00:53.961
easier to apply.

20
00:00:54.041 --> 00:00:55.663
Transport settings deserve review.

21
00:00:55.743 --> 00:00:59.354
TLS configuration, certificate validation,
mutual authentication where used,

22
00:00:59.434 --> 00:01:02.940
and service-to-service connection settings
should be documented and tested.

23
00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:05.951
The goal is not to make every developer a
cryptography specialist;

24
00:01:06.031 --> 00:01:09.067
it is to make secure transport assumptions
explicit and reviewable.

25
00:01:09.147 --> 00:01:11.397
Serialization is also a data protection
boundary.

26
00:01:11.477 --> 00:01:13.570
Response structs, JSON tags, database
models,

27
00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:17.209
and internal objects should not
automatically expose every field they contain.

28
00:01:17.289 --> 00:01:19.382
Go makes it easy to define small response
types,

29
00:01:19.462 --> 00:01:21.399
and that habit supports data minimization.

30
00:01:21.479 --> 00:01:25.090
The same discipline applies to metrics and
traces: record what helps operations,

31
00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:26.740
not everything the process can see.
